  "query_preview": "/Tuesday through Saturday/...\nThe upper-level trough digging across our area through Tuesday will \nreturn a cooling pattern for the remainder of the week. Valley rain \nand mountain snow showers will continue on the southwest ID side \nthrough Tuesday morning as it tapers off east by the afternoon. Snow \nlevels will drop to 4500-5500 feet by Tuesday morning. This will \nbring minor snow accumulation to the Central Idaho Mountains and the \nIdaho-Nevada border, with Tuesday night snowfall totals between 1 to \n3 inches. A ridge of high pressure will briefly build into the \nregion on Wednesday, bringing mostly dry conditions. \n\nNear to slightly below normal temperatures are expected to remain in \nplace throughout the long-term period as active patterns continue. \nThere is generally strong guidance agreement on a deep Pacific upper \nlow digging into the Pacific Northwest and Great Basin regions \nWednesday through Friday. There is still slight guidance uncertainty \nover the upper low's exact movement pattern going into Friday, but a \n50-80% chance of precipitation remains for Wednesday through Friday. \nThis active troughing pattern will bring a further cooldown. \nConsiderable guidance uncertainty remains for snow level trends from \nmidweek to the end of the week. There is potential for snow levels \nto drop down to valley floors Wednesday through Friday, bringing the \npossibility of light snow showers over valleys. For now, the current \nlong-range forecast has rain and/or rain/snow mix staying the \ndominant precipitation type for most lower elevation valleys through \nFriday. The low pressure system will steer eastward out of the \nregion by early Saturday, with an amplifying Pacific ridge building \nimmediately behind it. Saturday will see partly clearing skies and \ndrier conditions.",
